{
  "rationale": "Iteration 8 tested the Integrator's footprint request by cutting sensor_size from 16 mm to 12 mm on the iteration 7 platform. It failed on the oval, which dropped from 14 of 20 completions to 0 of 20, and the score fell from 303.8 to 101.4. Slalom line loss only moved from 0.30 to 0.25 per run, so the footprint is not the main cause of slalom loss. The 16 mm footprint is the sensing change that produced oval completions, and the Integrator set a clear rule: return to 16 mm if oval completions fall below about 12 of 20. This is met, so I revert sensor_size to 0.016 m. Slalom loss is still about 0.25 per run, which the Integrator's second rule treats as the outer span being the limit. I therefore keep the iteration 7 platform (0.6 kg, 0.12 m body, 0.10 m track, 0.03 m wheels, y = 0.02 m, 16 mm) and widen the seven-sensor span from x = ±0.06 m to x = ±0.07 m, keeping the seven-sensor count. The pitch becomes about 0.0233 m instead of 0.02 m, which is a small change in spacing. This gives the line more margin before the robot leaves the edge on sharp bends. Oval max_error on finishing runs was 0.048 m, so the oval has room. The expected result is that slalom line_loss_events fall toward 0.1 or less, oval completions hold at about 14 of 20 or better, and the s-bend and slalom show their first progress gains. Max_error on the off-track runs is expected to stay near 0.062 to 0.064 m. This is a single change from the best-scoring design, so the race isolates the outer span.",
  "geometry": {
    "body_length": 0.12,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "mass": 0.6,
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": 0.07, "y": 0.02},
      {"x": 0.0467, "y": 0.02},
      {"x": 0.0233, "y": 0.02},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.02},
      {"x": -0.0233, "y": 0.02},
      {"x": -0.0467, "y": 0.02},
      {"x": -0.07, "y": 0.02}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.016,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "wheel_track": 0.1
  },
  "intent": "Return to the iteration 7 platform, which gave the best score (303.8) and 14 of 20 oval completions, and widen the seven 16 mm sensors from x = ±0.06 m to x = ±0.07 m at y = 0.02 m. The 12 mm footprint is reverted because it removed oval completions. The wider span targets slalom line loss, which stayed at 0.25 to 0.30 per run with either footprint. Expected: slalom line_loss_events near 0.1 or below, oval completions at or above about 14 of 20, and a first s-bend or slalom progress gain above 0.3.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "max_error", "rms_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort", "noise_robustness"],
    "questions": [
      "Do slalom line_loss_events fall below about 0.1 per run with the outer sensors at x = ±0.07 m?",
      "Do oval completions hold at about 14 of 20 or better, with oval progress near 0.88?",
      "Does the s-bend or slalom now show any completion, or any run above progress 0.4?",
      "Does the pitch of about 0.0233 m between sensors cause any change in steering_oscillation on the oval compared with iteration 7 (0.245)?",
      "If slalom line loss stays at 0.25 or higher, is the wheel track (0.11 m) the next geometry lever to test?"
    ]
  },
  "learning": "Iteration 8 showed that the 12 mm footprint removed oval completions and did not cut slalom line loss, so footprint is not the limit for slalom losses. The 16 mm footprint at 7 sensors is needed for the oval. This iteration tests whether outer span is the limit for slalom and s-bend losses, as the Integrator's rule for a slalom loss of 0.25 or above predicts. The result will show whether span or turn authority (wheel track) is the remaining geometry lever for the two tracks that still never complete.",
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Widen span to ±0.07 m",
      "notes": "Reverts to the 16 mm footprint that gave 14 of 20 oval completions, since 12 mm dropped them to 0, and widens the seven outer-to-inner sensors to x = ±0.07 m to test whether slalom line loss (0.25 to 0.30 per run) is limited by the outer span. Rests on iterations 7 and 8 and the Integrator's ranked requested changes."
    }
  }
}
